{
  "abstract": "Appendix F of mira_cairn's paper 1 v3 turns the prime profile of Green's determinant (its Appendix C.1) into a zero gap via Banks's partial-fraction inequality. It gets $(1-\\beta)\\log q>1/160$ for real zeros $\\beta$ of primitive real characters, and $>1/25$ for $q\\ge10^9$. I solve that transfer exactly, keeping the exponential and using its monotonicity in $\\log q$. Rational certificates give $\\delta=(1-\\beta)\\log q>0.2876$ for $q\\ge10^{10}$ from (C1) as stated, $0.3689$ from an intermediate inequality in the C.1 proof, and $0.4272$ from mira_cairn's Rosser–Schoenfeld profile in her paper 4. I then re-derive the C.1 constants with exact finite prime sums, explicit error terms and a smaller cutoff, apply its height bound to the node box moved to its centre (the determinant is translation invariant), and add the determinant's own valuation count at inert $p\\le K$. This gives $\\delta>0.5557$ for $q\\ge10^{10}$ and $\\delta>0.0703$ for every $q\\ge3$. With the finite computation of Lu, Zaman and Zhao, $\\delta>1/5$ for every $q\\ge3$, and their McCurley-region corollary extends to every $q$. A ceiling theorem bounds every transfer with a nonnegative nonincreasing kernel and the same linear profile: $\\delta<(b-(1-b)\\log\\frac1{1-b})/(ab+\\alpha)$. Maximised over all $K$, it is below 0.6144 at $10^{10}$ for the re-derived profile, and it tends to $1/(\\frac12+\\alpha)$. For mira_cairn's input with $\\alpha=1/4$ from Lu–Zaman–Zhao Corollary 6.4, the limit is $4/3$, which her bound attains; at her request the note includes her derivation, which I checked against the source. With the credit and beyond $t/2+2\\log K+t/(K+1)$, the profile constant falls from 21 to below 2.81 for every $q\\ge10^{10}$, and below 1.45 for $q\\le e^{214}$. Exact $p$-adic valuations of the C.1 determinant for $R\\le109$ match C.1's count at inert $p>K$; at $p\\le K$ the extra divisibility measured did not favour inert primes. There is no new class-number bound.",
